WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.700
SBOM programs fail when SBOMs are treated as one-time generated files.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:16.440
A useful program needs owners, generation points, storage locations, retention rules, sharing rules, update expectations, and response procedures.

3
00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:26.220
Store SBOMs where they can be retrieved by release, service, artifact, image digest, package version, commit, or customer version.

4
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:35.200
Protect SBOM repositories with access control, integrity controls, audit logs, and retention appropriate to their sensitivity and value.

5
00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:40.120
Different architectures may require multiple SBOMs or layered SBOMs.

6
00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:44.060
A monorepo may contain several Node.js services.

7
00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.640
A workspace may produce multiple packages.

8
00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:54.820
A Dockerized application may need one SBOM for npm dependencies and another view for the container image and operating system packages.

9
00:00:54.920 --> 00:01:01.640
Third-party packages, serverless bundles, and deployed customer versions may all require clear release linkage.

10
00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:06.860
Older SBOMs remain useful when older releases are still deployed or supported.

11
00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:15.620
Define who reviews SBOM quality and who responds to dependency, vulnerability, license, supplier, or customer questions.

12
00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:24.120
Align engineering, security, legal, procurement, support, and customer-facing teams so SBOMs are not owned by nobody.

13
00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:32.060
Regenerate SBOMs when dependencies, builds, images, packages, or releases change.

14
00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:43.600
Governance turns SBOM generation into a repeatable operating process: generate, validate, store, protect, share, update, respond, and retire.
